  "worked_solution": "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of televisions produced with defects. Since the average rate is $5$ televisions with defects per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of televisions with defects can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=1-P(X\\le 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{0}{e}^{-5}}{0!}+\\frac{{5}^{1}{e}^{-5}}{1!}+\\frac{{5}^{2}{e}^{-5}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $2$ defective televisions in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.125 \\\\  & =0.875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
